    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d404">

                <pb facs="0511" n="497" sameAs="frus:frus1947v06/pg497"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 PW (Peace)/8–2047: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Embassy in
                        Australia </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-08-22T18:00:00-04:00">August 22, 1947—6
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>199. Urtel 247, Aug 20.<anchor n="35" xml:id="d404-f1"/> No definite date for
                    calling peace conference been set in view of fact impossible to predict nature
                    or date of Soviet reply to US <hi rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> of Aug
                    12. You may tell External Affairs that in view of shortness of time it seems
                    unlikely conference can convene by Sept 10.<anchor n="36" xml:id="d404-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lovett</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d404-f1" n="35" xml:id="d404-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d404-f2" n="36" xml:id="d404-f2-content">
                <p>Telegram 250, August 25, 1 p.m., from Canberra, reported Dr. Evatt’s opinion that
                    it was urgently necessary to hold the peace conference before the UNGA meeting,
                    even if only a few days beforehand, and that the meeting should take place even
                    if the Soviet Government refused to attend (740.0011 PW (Peace)/8–2547).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
